# CLI Colored Logging Conventions

When writing or modifying CLI output/logging code in this Go project, follow these conventions.

## Format

All log-style messages use this format:

```
[level] - message text
```

- The brackets `[` `]` and the message text are **never colored**.
- Only the level word itself (`info`, `warning`, `error`) is colored.

## Colors

| Level     | Color  |
|-----------|--------|
| `info`    | Blue   |
| `warning` | Yellow |
| `error`   | Red    |

## Implementation

Use `github.com/fatih/color` for colorization. Define `SprintFunc()` helpers per level and only wrap the level string, not the whole line:

```go
var (
	infoColor  = color.New(color.FgBlue).SprintFunc()
	warnColor  = color.New(color.FgYellow).SprintFunc()
	errorColor = color.New(color.FgRed).SprintFunc()
)

func logMsg(level, msg string, args ...interface{}) {
	var coloredLevel string
	switch level {
	case "info":
		coloredLevel = infoColor(level)
	case "warning":
		coloredLevel = warnColor(level)
	case "error":
		coloredLevel = errorColor(level)
	default:
		coloredLevel = level
	}
	fmt.Printf("[%s] - %s\n", coloredLevel, fmt.Sprintf(msg, args...))
}

func Info(msg string, args ...interface{})    { logMsg("info", msg, args...) }
func Warning(msg string, args ...interface{}) { logMsg("warning", msg, args...) }
func Error(msg string, args ...interface{})   { logMsg("error", msg, args...) }
```

## Rules to enforce

- Do not introduce a second colored-logging library (e.g. `lipgloss`, `termenv`, `aec`) — stick to `fatih/color` for consistency.
- Do not color the message body, punctuation, or brackets — only the level word.
- Centralize level-to-color mapping in one place (e.g. a `logging` or `ui` package) rather than inlining `color.New(...)` calls at call sites.
- Respect `NO_COLOR` and non-TTY output. Rely on `fatih/color`'s built-in `color.NoColor` detection rather than reimplementing TTY checks, unless the project already has its own detection logic.
- Keep level names lowercase (`info`, `warning`, `error`) to match the existing format exactly.
- If a new level is added (e.g. `debug`, `fatal`), follow the same pattern: pick a semantic color, add it to the switch statement, and only colorize the level word.
